To fix this issue, the primary problem lies in the incorrect calculation of `dataLength`. The GT06 protocol specifies that the `length` field includes the bytes from the length byte itself up to, but not including, the checksum and termination bytes. Thus, the correct `dataLength` should be `length - 2` instead of `length - 5`. This adjustment ensures proper parsing of the message data, leading to the correct alarm type being read and set.

```java
    private Object decodeBasic(Channel channel, SocketAddress remoteAddress, ByteBuf buf) {

        int length = buf.readUnsignedByte();
        int dataLength = length - 2; // Corrected from length - 5 to length - 2
        int type = buf.readUnsignedByte();

        Position position = new Position(getProtocolName());
        DeviceSession deviceSession = null;
        if (type != MSG_LOGIN) {
            deviceSession = getDeviceSession(channel, remoteAddress);
            if (deviceSession == null) {
                return null;
            }
            position.setDeviceId(deviceSession.getDeviceId());
            if (!deviceSession.contains(DeviceSession.KEY_TIMEZONE)) {
                deviceSession.set(DeviceSession.KEY_TIMEZONE, getTimeZone(deviceSession.getDeviceId()));
            }
